// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-045
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-045
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-043
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-045
// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-045
//
// D-181's rules for the `thread` modifier. A thread body owns an executor
// and the arena its task frames come from (D-034), which is what makes it a
// different KIND of function from a task rather than the same one used
// differently — so it is `async` (D-071: a thread's entry point runs on its
// own executor, and `await` is how anything on one waits), its success type
// is `NIL` (it starts through the spawn form, and only its error reaches
// the join — a value would have nowhere to go), and neither `main` nor
// `failsafe` may be one: `main` already owns the program's first executor,
// and `failsafe` runs on the trapping thread as a plain call (D-063). The
// last declaration earns FOUR refusals between D-177 and D-181 — async
// failsafe, thread failsafe, and a non-NIL success — which is the rules
// composing rather than overlapping: each names a different thing wrong.
mod:thread_rules;

thread func:not_async = NIL(int32:_~v) {
    pass NIL;
};

thread async func:gives_a_value = int32(int32:v) {
    pass v;
};

async func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { exit 0i32; };

thread async func:failsafe = int32(Error:_~e) { exit 1i32; };
